(As you mention, the messages about libpq-fe are harmless; they should
simply stop the server building, not the main executable.)

The first thing to check (to help diagnose the problem) would be the
versions of the relevant commands:

nm --version
gcc --version
ld --version

and the specific NetHack 4 version you're building:

git log --pretty=oneline HEAD^1..

Because the problem is in finding libraries, try running the command
that aimake uses to locate libraries yourself (from the build
directory):

gcc -rdynamic -Wl,-t -L/usr/local/lib -lz aimake/aimake1.o

(Also try with -ljansson instead of -lz.)

Finally, post the output of the following commands (from the build
directory), to see what aimake itself thinks about the library location:

perl -MData::Dumper -MStorable=retrieve -e 'print Dumper(retrieve("aimake.objects")->{hash_by_pair}->{"find_libraries searchlib:z"})'
perl -MData::Dumper -MStorable=retrieve -e 'print Dumper(retrieve("aimake.objects")->{xuse_by_pair}->{"find_libraries searchlib:z"})'

(I should really add an option to aimake to be abe to do that via
aimake rather than requiring the Perl oneliner.)

That should be enough information to at least make a start towards
diagnosing the problem.

